No, raising the pool size probably won't help. `withClient` in `db.js` leaks a client whenever `fn` throws.

`client.release()` runs only after `await fn(client)` succeeds, and there is no `try/finally`. Each failed call permanently removes one connection from the pool. A larger pool would only take longer to drain.

The log is consistent with that. `idle=0` at every sample, and `waiting` only grows (7, 11, 14, 19, 23, 28). A pool that was merely too small would show idle connections at least briefly as load dipped.

The fix is to release in a `finally` block:

```js
async function withClient(fn) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    return await fn(client);
  } finally {
    client.release();
  }
}
```

`pool.log` covers only 14:02:11 to 14:04:42, about two and a half minutes, not the last hour. That is too short to rule out a legitimate load spike or slow queries holding connections. If callers elsewhere use `pool.connect()` directly, they could have the same leak. I only had `db.js` to look at.

To confirm, check `pg_stat_activity` for connections stuck `idle in transaction`, or see whether `total=20 idle=0` persists after traffic drops. A real capacity problem would recover when load falls, but a leak won't.

